    "body": "### What happens?\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT col ... ORDER BY col DESC LIMIT N` returns only **1 row** instead of N rows when the table is large enough. The bug is specific to the combination of `DISTINCT` + `ORDER BY DESC` + `LIMIT` (with `LIMIT` below a certain threshold).\n\n### To Reproduce\n\n```sql\n-- DuckDB v1.5.1 & v1.5.2 on Windows, 17.6M rows table with 8643 distinct DATE values\n\n-- Bug: returns only 1 row\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date DESC LIMIT 3000;\n-- Result: 1 row (expected: 3000)\n\n-- All these work correctly:\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date ASC  LIMIT 3000;  -- OK: 3000 rows\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date DESC;            -- OK: 8643 rows (no LIMIT)\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date DESC LIMIT 5000; -- OK: 5000 rows (large LIMIT)\nSELECT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq GROUP BY date ORDER BY date DESC LIMIT 3000; -- OK: 3000 rows (GROUP BY workaround)\n```\n\nThe threshold where the bug stops occurring is between ~3400–3500 in my test case (8643 distinct dates, 17.6M rows). The bug is **deterministic** — same result every run with fresh connections.\n\n### OS:\n\nWindows 11\n\n### DuckDB Version:\n\nv1.5.1 and v1.5.2 (Python package)\n\n### DuckDB Client:\n\nPython\n\n### Hardware:\n\n14 CPU threads\n\n### Full Name:\n\nxuyang7952\n\n### Affiliation:\n\nPersonal\n\n### Did you include all relevant configuration (e.g., CPU architecture, Linux distribution) to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [ ] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all code required to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [ ] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all relevant data sets for reproducing the issue?\n\nNo - Other reason (please specify in the issue body)",
